# BUG-295 (b) — a module imported by more than one module AND declaring module-level
# state is NAMED at the end of compilation (Sean's ask, 2026-08-18).
#
# WHAT THE NOTE SAYS WAS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED, AND THE OBVIOUS GUESS WAS WRONG. A
# diamond leaf's initialiser does NOT run once per importer: the root emits one
# `_initModuleVars()` per entry of a DEDUPLICATED transitive list, so it runs exactly
# once, before main. Warning about repeated initialisation would have sent a reader
# hunting a bug that does not exist.
#
# What IS true is the sharing, and this fixture demonstrates it rather than asserting
# it: the LEFT parent bumps twice, the RIGHT parent reads, and it sees 2. One instance,
# shared. That is the direct consequence of "first import wins".
#
# The silent control is bug295_diamond_test.zbr — a shared leaf with NO state must
# produce nothing, or this note is noise. Real-world check: the selfhost compiling
# ITSELF is silent, because CodeGen (14 top-level vars) has exactly one importer per
# compilation.
use bug295_shared_state_left exposing leftBump
use bug295_shared_state_right exposing rightRead

def main()
    leftBump()
    leftBump()
    print("bug295-shared=${rightRead()}")
